ID: B2-Ch04a-Intro
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_cell == 1) {
The stone trapdoor overhead grinds open, and the counterweight platform hoists us through the bedrock ceiling. Near our division level, the upper slab slides back. The platform shudders to a halt, then immediately reverses course, descending back toward the flooded tunnels. We step off onto the flagstones as the trapdoor thuds shut, cutting off the subterranean draft.

Looking around, this isn't Leila's cell. It's mine. Good thing I picked cell three from the intake clerk; if I'd taken five instead, yesterday's new inmate would be occupying this cot.

Water streams from our clothes, pooling across the stone.

"We need dry clothes," I say, digging the iron room key from my pocket. "And I want to check my notebook for anything on those cavern beasts. Give me a couple of hours and I'll come find you."

Leila nods.

"See you later," blue letters hang in the air before dissolving, and she slips out into the corridor.

I turn straight to my backpack for dry trousers and a clean shirt.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_cell != 1) {
The stone trapdoor overhead grinds open, and the counterweight platform hoists us through the bedrock ceiling. Near our division level, the upper slab slides back. The platform shudders to a halt, then immediately reverses course, descending back toward the flooded tunnels. We step off onto the flagstones as the trapdoor thuds shut, cutting off the subterranean draft.

"What—how did you get in here?" a voice cracks behind us. "Where did you come from? Why are you soaking wet?"

Leila and I spin around. The lift didn't drop us in Leila's cell. It brought us to cell three—the room the clerk offered me at intake, now occupied by the mage locked up yesterday.

He stands pressed against the wall, sweat glistening on his forehead, his hands tucked hastily behind his back.

"Pardon the intrusion," I say, wringing water from my sleeve. "Took a wrong turn during a swim. We'll be out of your hair the second you turn that latch."

"A swim?" His voice climbs an octave. "You were breaking out. Give me one reason not to yell for the guards right now."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Technically, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Technically, v_checkpoint_rich = 0) if (v_b2_ch2_cell == 1)
choice(""If you let us go, I won't ask anything about that thing you are hiding behind your back."", B2-Ch04a-Hiding, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Hiding, v_checkpoint_rich = 0) if (v_b2_ch2_cell != 1)
choice(""Go ahead. The arena's owner already knows what happened."", B2-Ch04a-Owner, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Owner, v_checkpoint_rich = 0) if (v_b2_ch2_cell != 1)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Hiding
TEXT:

"If you let us go, I won't ask anything about that thing you are hiding behind your back."

"What thing?" he snaps. "I'm not hiding anything!"

"It's not going to work," blue letters ignite in front of Leila.

"What is that?" The mage blinks at the glowing script. "How are you doing that? What won't work?"

Leila crouches, picking up a palm-sized contraption off the floor—a jagged cluster of scrap metal and soldered wire cobbled together with zero finesse. She steps forward, holding it inches from his nose.

"This," the blue text reads. "This is what's not going to work."

"No idea what you're talking about," he bluffs, eyes shifting. "Just rubbish."

"It's a booster," Leila writes. "Built to force a temporary surge in physical stats. And whatever you have behind your back is supposed to bridge your aura so you can channel mana into it over time."

"How did you—"

A fresh line of text cuts him off.

"You can't take this into the arena. Detectors will catch it the second it draws power. Without an aura-sync casing, the magical discharge when you switch it on will light up every sensor in a five-mile radius. And even if you bypass them, the wiring is so crude that ten hours of charging will buy you two minutes of output."

"Two minutes in the pit is everything!" the mage shouts, veins bulging in his neck. "Two minutes is breathing versus a broken neck! You've never stood out there! You don't know what it's like! Any edge I can scrape together, I'm taking!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Meeting, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Meeting)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Owner
TEXT:

"Go ahead. The arena's owner already knows what happened."

"Liar," the mage says. "You haven't even seen the owner."

"It's not going to work," blue letters ignite in front of Leila.

"What is that?" The mage blinks at the glowing script. "How are you doing that? What won't work?"

Leila crouches, picking up a palm-sized contraption off the floor—a jagged cluster of scrap metal and soldered wire cobbled together with zero finesse. She steps forward, holding it inches from his nose.

"This," the blue text reads. "This is what's not going to work."

"No idea what you're talking about," he bluffs, eyes shifting. "Just rubbish."

"It's a booster," Leila writes. "Built to force a temporary surge in physical stats. And whatever you have behind your back is supposed to bridge your aura so you can channel mana into it over time."

"How did you—"

A fresh line of text cuts him off.

"You can't take this into the arena. Detectors will catch it the second it draws power. Without an aura-sync casing, the magical discharge when you switch it on will light up every sensor in a five-mile radius. And even if you bypass them, the wiring is so crude that ten hours of charging will buy you two minutes of output."

"Two minutes in the pit is everything!" the mage shouts, veins bulging in his neck. "Two minutes is breathing versus a broken neck! You've never stood out there! You don't know what it's like! Any edge I can scrape together, I'm taking!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Meeting, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Meeting)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Meeting
TEXT:

"Don't say I didn't warn you," Leila writes, pressing the metal scrap into his palm.

He stares at the device for a long beat, jaw clenched, before fishing an iron key from his pocket. He steps past us and turns the lock, pulling the door open a crack.

"We never spoke," he says, looking hard between the two of us. "Understand? Now get out."

"Gladly," I say, slipping out into the corridor with Leila right behind me.

The heavy door thuds shut. We head down the hallway toward our cells.

"You identified that junk fast," I say in a low voice.

"I spent years in my father's workshop at the forest cabin," glowing text answers at my shoulder. "You pick things up watching him assemble components."

"Think he can modify it before tomorrow?"

"Maybe," Leila writes. "If he doesn't sleep. He can't charge the core while the casing is open, and something that inefficient needs ten hours on a steady feed to hold any charge at all."

"Let's get dry," I say, reaching my door. "I'm going through my notes to see what they say about those cavern beasts. Give me a couple of hours and I'll come find you."

Leila nods.

"See you later," her text reads, and she heads down the hall to her room.

I step inside, lock the door, and dig through my pack for dry trousers and a clean shirt.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Technically, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Technically)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Technically
TEXT:

Once I change and leave the wet garments over the bench, I pull out my notebook and flip through my sketches and notes.

An hour in, the transceiver on the cot buzzes.

"Barry? Are you alive?" Flower's voice bursts through, pitched high with panic.

I can tell it's her and not Illuna from the breathless rush.

"Still breathing," I say. "We reached the cells about an hour ago."

"Thank the gods!" Flower breathes out. "When we heard what happened, I was terrified—especially after Fyron left you down there. Petal was frantic too!"

"Really?" I say, raising an eyebrow. "She told you that?"

"Well, technically," Flower says, "what she said was that if you couldn't survive a minor scrape after entering the tournament, you deserved whatever happened to you. But she didn't mean it!"

choice(""Oh yeah? Well, you tell her that her blue eyes make her look fat."", B2-Ch04a-Fat, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Fat)
choice(""It's okay. I know she means well!"", B2-Ch04a-Means, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Means)
choice(""What about Arraka? Was she not worried about me?"", B2-Ch04a-Worried, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Worried)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Fat
TEXT:

"Oh yeah? Well, you tell her that her blue eyes make her look fat."

"I can hear every word, Barry," Illuna's voice cuts in, flat and frigid.

"Wait—do her blue eyes really make me look fat?" Flower pipes up.

"Hey, mortal!" Arraka's voice shrills through, cutting Flower off. "One thing's been nagging at me since the tunnels. How in the hell did you talk that troll into letting you walk? The moment his aura moved on you, I thought you were paste."

"Velgos?" I ask. "The one who stays upright without his head? The arena's owner stepped in. He wants a spectacle for the crowd, so they're putting us against the troll in round two."

"Hah!" Arraka cackles. "I'll make sure to get front-row seats!"

"Be careful, Barry," Flower says. "We can't do anything to help you inside the arena."

"I know," I say. "I'll manage. Thanks for checking in."

After signing off, I spend another hour cross-referencing creature lore in my notebook, then reach for the transceiver to brief the others in their divisions. When I get to Kate, I bring up her old companion from the Beacon.

"Hey, Kate," I say. "Back at the Beacon of Hope, do you remember a mute lessathi girl with silver hair? Or maybe her hair was black back then, I'm not certain."

Dead silence on the line. I can't even hear her draw breath.

"Where did you hear that name?" Kate demands after a long freeze.

"So you do remember her. Were the two of you close, or—"

"Tell me how you know her!" Kate's voice cracks with sudden intensity.

"She's in my division," I say quickly. "I met her yesterday. She's alive, Kate."

Another heavy pause fills the link.

"Are you certain?" Kate's voice trembles, guarded and tight. "How do you know it's her? It could be a trick. Anyone can bleach their hair and pretend to be mute. The Leila I knew died years ago."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Change, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Change)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Means
TEXT:

"It's okay. I know she means well!"

"Hey, mortal!" Arraka's voice shrills through, cutting in. "One thing's been nagging at me since the tunnels. How in the hell did you talk that troll into letting you walk? The moment his aura moved on you, I thought you were paste."

