choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Change, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Change)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Worried
TEXT:

"What about Arraka? Was she not worried about me?"

"Worried sick!" Arraka shrieks with laughter. "I worry every single day! I pray continuously for someone to cure your incurable stupidity, but the universe refuses to deliver. Don't lose hope, though—I'm sure someone in that pit will put you out of your misery soon enough!"

"Your warmth is overwhelming," I tell the amulet.

"Any time, mortal!" Arraka says. "Now tell me—how in the hell did you talk that troll into letting you walk? The moment his aura moved on you, I thought you were paste."

"Velgos?" I ask. "The one who stays upright without his head? The arena's owner stepped in. He wants a spectacle for the crowd, so they're putting us against the troll in round two."

"Hah!" Arraka cackles. "I'll make sure to get front-row seats!"

"Be careful, Barry," Flower says. "We can't do anything to help you inside the arena."

"I know," I say. "I'll manage. Thanks for checking in."

After signing off, I spend another hour cross-referencing creature lore in my notebook, then reach for the transceiver to brief the others in their divisions. When I get to Kate, I bring up her old companion from the Beacon.

"Hey, Kate," I say. "Back at the Beacon of Hope, do you remember a mute lessathi girl with silver hair? Or maybe her hair was black back then, I'm not certain."

Dead silence on the line. I can't even hear her draw breath.

"Where did you hear that name?" Kate demands after a long freeze.

"So you do remember her. Were the two of you close, or—"

"Tell me how you know her!" Kate's voice cracks with sudden intensity.

"She's in my division," I say quickly. "I met her yesterday. She's alive, Kate."

Another heavy pause fills the link.

"Are you certain?" Kate's voice trembles, guarded and tight. "How do you know it's her? It could be a trick. Anyone can bleach their hair and pretend to be mute. The Leila I knew died years ago."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Change, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Change)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Change
TEXT:

"Why?" I say. "Did you find her body?"

"...No," Kate says. "I did not."

"Well, then," I say, "see her for yourself. You'll know the minute you look at her."

"All right, Barry," Kate says, her tone guarded. "I'll meet her. But be careful. There were plenty of lessathi in that place who knew our names. The information could have gotten out any number of ways."

"I'll be careful," I say. "See you at the arena tomorrow. Don't be late."

"See you tomorrow," Kate says, and cuts the transmission.

Once I finish calling the others to pass along creature notes and weaknesses, I head down the corridor to Leila's room. I knock three times. The door swings open, and Leila stands there in the same clothes from the tunnels. They are no longer dripping, but the fabric is still dark and cold with moisture.

"You didn't change?" I ask.

"I don't have a change of clothes," Leila writes. "I wasn't carrying a pack when the guards took me."

"Why didn't you say something? You could have borrowed some of mine until yours dried."

"I didn't want to be a bother," Leila writes.

"What about food?" I ask, frowning. "Have you eaten anything since you arrived?"

"The guards provide rations," Leila writes. "Hardtack and water. Just enough. It's fine."

"Hardtack won't cut it before a fight," I say. "Wait here. I'll bring you clothes and food."

"You don't have to—" blue letters flicker before Leila, but I turn on my heel and head back to my cell, grabbing a spare shirt, trousers, dried beef, and a water skin from my pack.

Back at her doorway, I hand over the bundle.

"Put these on before you catch a cold right before the games."

"Thank you..." Leila writes, taking the bundle, and pulls the door shut.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Troll, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Troll)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Troll
TEXT:

A few minutes later, the door creaks open. She stands in my clothes—the shirt sleeves drooping well past her fingertips, the trousers pooling over her sandals and trailing along the stone floor.

"These clothes are... a little big," Leila writes.

"They're dry," I say. "You won't be fighting in them anyway. Let's head inside—we need to talk through what I found."

We step inside. For the next fifteen minutes, I walk her through the notes and creature classifications from my book. She sits on the edge of the cot, chewing slowly on a piece of jerky, listening to every detail. When I finish, she sets the food aside and starts writing.

"From what you're saying," Leila writes, "our biggest problem is the troll and his regeneration."

"Right," I say. "Trolls knit tissue fast enough to survive a pierced heart, but functioning after decapitation defies everything in the texts. In theory, severing the head should drop the body instantly. The fact that the torso walked over and picked the head back up breaks every known rule of anatomy."

"He didn't regrow the head," Leila writes. "The severed head kept talking from the dirt, and the body had to feel around to retrieve it."

"Which means the sensory connection didn't sever with the neck," I say. "If the body still relies on the head's eyes and ears, maybe we cut the head off again, grab it, and blindfold it or bag it. That leaves the body blind."

"He won't give us that opening in the arena," Leila writes. "I used surprise before. Now he knows my speed and he'll guard his throat."

"Standard trolls die by fire or beheading," I say. "If beheading won't stick, fire is our only option. But getting open flame onto the arena floor is next to impossible, and his armor has anti-magic enchantments that dampen heat. We'd have to crack that plating before mundane fire could even scorch the skin."

"Should we talk to the other members of our division?" Leila writes. "Maybe they have ideas."

choice(""I suppose it couldn't hurt to hear their opinions on the matter."", B2-Ch04a-Matter, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Matter, v_b2_ch4_inform = 1)
choice(""No, let them find their own information. We almost died to get ours."", B2-Ch04a-Information, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Information, v_b2_ch4_inform = 0)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Matter
TEXT:

"I suppose it couldn't hurt to hear their opinions on the matter."

Leila nods.

"All right," I say. "Let's knock on their doors and gather in the common room."

I turn toward the corridor, but a tug on my sleeve stops me. Glowing blue letters hover in the air: "Wait."

The letters dissolve and reform.

"Can we do this later?"

"Later?"

"After my clothes dry..." Leila writes, looking down at the bunched sleeves, a faint plea in her eyes.

"Right," I say. "Makes sense."

I lean against the doorframe for a moment.

"On second thought, tomorrow's recreation block might work better anyway. The tournament doesn't start until the following morning, so we'll have time to coordinate. Banging on doors like town criers will just put their guards up. They'll be easier to corner when we're all sitting in the same hall."

Leila nods.

"I'll head back to my cell and dig through everything I wrote on regeneration," I say. "If we understand how the troll's body keeps functioning, we can break it. Let me know if you come up with anything."

"I will," Leila writes.

"See you later."

"See you later," her script hangs as I step out into the hallway.

Back in my cell, I shut the heavy latch and sit on the cot with my notebook. My thoughts drift back to the lessathi emissary and the deal with the king. I haven't mentioned a word of it to Daren or the others. Should I?

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Knowledge, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Knowledge)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Information
TEXT:

"No, let them find their own information. We almost died to get ours."

"Understood," Leila writes.

"So the troll remains our primary target," I say.

Leila nods, and I pause to think through the next step.

"I'll head back to my cell and dig through everything I wrote on regeneration," I say. "If we understand how the troll's body keeps functioning, we can break it. Let me know if you come up with anything."

"I will," Leila writes.

"See you later."

"See you later," her script hangs as I step out into the hallway.

Back in my cell, I shut the heavy latch and sit on the cot with my notebook. My thoughts drift back to the lessathi emissary and the deal with the king. I haven't mentioned a word of it to Daren or the others. Should I?

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Knowledge, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Knowledge)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Knowledge
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_deal == 3) {
No. Definitely not. Daren and Kate would shut it down immediately, and the fallout would blow the group apart. Better to keep my mouth shut. Still, I wonder who the last-minute addition to our division will be, and how they expect him to solve anything. One lessathi isn't going to clear an entire arena of monsters single-handed.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_deal != 3) {
No. No point loading them with panic before the games. If I tell them, it'll be after the tournament, when everyone is safe.

}

Flipping through the notebook's dog-eared pages, my thumb catches on a passage I skimmed past earlier. It references an obscure treatise arguing that extreme tissue regeneration stems from structural differences in a creature's magical aura rather than pure biology.

Eiden's lecture outside the city walls comes rushing back: warp a person's aura far enough, and biological aging halts entirely. If that holds true, an engineered or mutated aura could drive cell replication at impossible speeds. And if the aura extends beyond the severed flesh, could it maintain the nerve bridge between body and head across open air?

Aura radius isn't infinite. What happens if the severed head is carried fifty paces away from the torso? Does the connection snap? Does the body drop lifeless? If Leila can take his head off again, putting distance between the pieces might kill him for good.

I spend the rest of the evening cross-checking notes on aura mechanics, but mainstream scholarship barely tracks basic mana density, let alone aura-linked biology. Most academies dismiss the entire concept as ungrounded theory, leaving the few researchers who pursued it without funding.

By nightfall, all I have is a speculative hypothesis built on another hypothesis, propped up by an offhand remark from a stillwater. Better than nothing.

I slide the notebook into my backpack and prepare for sleep. I'll have more time to study tomorrow.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Cards, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Cards)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Cards
TEXT:

Late that evening, Leila knocked on my door to return my clothes. I asked if she'd thought of any counters, but she only suggested crushing the troll's head once it's severed, hoping the brain can't regenerate if it's pulped.

At noon the next day, our recreation block begins. When I step out into the common room, everyone is sitting in a circle on the stone floor. Wilhelm stands over them, shuffling a grease-stained deck of cards.

"You're late," Wilhelm says. "Take a seat. We were about to deal without you."

"What's going on here?" I ask.

"Wondering why we're suddenly cordial after ignoring each other for days?" Wilhelm smiles. "The arena owner just paid us a visit."

"Venard?"

"In the flesh," Wilhelm says. "The guards reported that our division hasn't been building camaraderie during recreation hours. He came down personally to inform us that if we don't present a coordinated front, he'll feed us to the beasts one by one instead of letting us fight as a unit. Today is our final chance to prove we can cooperate."

"So you broke out cards."

"One has to start somewhere," Wilhelm says, bridging the deck with practiced fingers. "And I happened to have a deck."

"Fair enough. What are we playing?"

"We're playing shut your mouth and take your cards," one of the thugs growls, glaring up from the floor.

choice(""If the game is called 'shut your mouth', then why are your lips moving?"", B2-Ch04a-Lips, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Lips)
choice(""That didn't answer my question."", B2-Ch04a-Answer, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Answer)
choice(""I've never heard of this game before. Could you please teach me the rules?"", B2-Ch04a-Rules, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Rules)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Lips
TEXT:

"If the game is called 'shut your mouth', then why are your lips moving?"

"What did you say to me, you little—" The thug pushes up from the flagstones, hand dropping toward his belt, but Wilhelm steps in between us.

"Easy," Wilhelm says, resting a palm on the thug's shoulder. "We're supposed to be a team, remember?"

"Get your filthy hand off me, gutter trash," the thug snaps.

For a split second, Wilhelm's pleasant smile vanishes, replaced by cold, lethal venom. Before anyone can react, Bruce lets out a booming bark of laughter.

"Gutter trash?" Bruce chuckles. "And what does that make you? The lost heir of Olmnar?"

"Close," the newly arrived mage mutters. "Disinherited nobility. Look at that ragged silk around his neck—that's the crest of House Tilirius."

"House Tilirius?" Bruce smirks. "Ah, so you're highborn scum. My mistake!"

He laughs again.

"Can we return to the deck?" Wilhelm asks, his voice smooth once more. "The guards are still watching through the grate."

"Nobody wants your cards, Wilbert," Bruce grunts. "The owner told us to talk, so we're talking. He didn't mandate card tricks. Play solitaire if you love them so much."

"My name is Wilhelm, not Wilbert," Wilhelm says, the pitch dropping low and sharp.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Strategy, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Strategy)


