We all look over at Leila. She stands with her arms crossed, glaring at the group.

"Right, well..." Bruce rubs the back of his neck. "Can't argue with that."

"Agreed," Wilhelm says.

"And discuss what, exactly?" the mage grumbles. "The guards haven't given us a scrap of intelligence. All we know is that round one involves mythical beasts."

Leila looks straight at me.

She's prompting me to lay out what we discovered in the tunnels, as we discussed. It's still not too late to pull back, though.

}
choice("Ask Wilhelm if he overheard the guards say anything about the arena event", B2-Ch04a-Overheard, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Overheard, v_b2_ch4_inform2 = 0) if (v_b2_ch2_threatened != 2)
choice("Ask if somebody overheard the guards say anything about the arena event", B2-Ch04a-Overheard, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Overheard, v_b2_ch4_inform2 = 0) if (v_b2_ch2_threatened == 2)
choice("Tell everyone about what we found out in the caverns", B2-Ch04a-Caverns, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Caverns, v_b2_ch4_inform2 = 1, v_b2_ch4_inform3 = 0)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Caverns
TEXT:

"Actually, there is some information you should hear," I say. "Brace yourselves, because it's a long breakdown."

I lay out our descent into the underground tunnels and the creatures housed below. I omit the floor trap mechanism, Flower, Illuna, and the transceiver entirely, and scrub any mention of stat boosters or our lessathi blood. Beyond that, I describe the menagerie in detail—behaviors, attack patterns, weak points—along with my working hypothesis on the troll's aura-linked regeneration.

"Valuable intel," Wilhelm says, tapping his chin. "I overheard guards talking about a troll mercenary named Velgos in round two, but I had no idea he possessed that kind of resilience. They also mentioned manticores, which weren't in your tunnels. If your notebook covers them, we should review that as well."

#if(v_b2_ch2_heard == 1) {
"I already looked them up," I say. "Overheard the guards discussing manticores and giant scorpions a while back. I'll walk you through the specifics later."

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_heard == 0) {
"I'll look into them," I say. "Thanks for the heads-up."

}
"That trapdoor you fell through," Bruce says, leaning forward. "Did you manage to trigger it again?"

"I tried several times," Leila writes. "The mechanism locked."

"Mind if we check your cell?" Bruce asks. "Maybe one of us can pry it loose."

"No," Leila writes firmly. "Nobody enters my room."

"Except the stillwater?" the mage sneers.

"Except the stillwater," Leila writes without blinking.

"So the troll is our main hurdle," Wilhelm says. "I have no background in magic theory, so this aura concept sounds far-fetched to me."

"Leila's plan to pulp the skull makes more sense," Bruce grunts. "A brain can't regenerate if it's smeared across the floor."

"Regardless," Wilhelm says, "we can't handle these threats divided. We don't have to like each other, but we need a truce through the tournament. Survival requires it."

Everyone nods, some more begrudgingly than others. We spend the rest of the recreation block mapping out battle formations against known beasts and theoretical scenarios. We even agree to reconvene later in the evening after everyone reviews the notes on their own.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Leaving, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Leaving)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Overheard
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch4_inform == 0 && v_b2_ch2_threatened != 2) {
"Hey, Wilhelm," I say. "Did you happen to catch the guards talking about the matchups?"

"As a matter of fact, yes," Wilhelm says. "Last night I overheard two patrolmen mention harpies and manticores for the opening round. Might have heard gargoyles too, though I'm not certain."

"Better than expected," Bruce grunts. "Gargoyles are rough, but they're nothing like facing a hydra."

"A hydra doesn't fly," the mage retorts. "I'd take a hydra on solid ground over winged predators when we have no ranged weapons or battle magic."

"That's not all," Wilhelm adds. "They spoke about the second round. A notorious troll mercenary named Velgos. They claim he's fought in this coliseum before and rivals the undefeated champion."

}
#if(v_b2_ch4_inform == 1 && v_b2_ch2_threatened != 2) {
"Hey, Wilhelm," I say. "Did you happen to catch the guards talking about the matchups?"

Seeing that I've decided against sharing the cavern findings, Leila casts a disappointed glance my way, but keeps quiet.

"As a matter of fact, yes," Wilhelm says. "Last night I overheard two patrolmen mention harpies and manticores for the opening round. Might have heard gargoyles too, though I'm not certain."

"Better than expected," Bruce grunts. "Gargoyles are rough, but they're nothing like facing a hydra."

"A hydra doesn't fly," the mage retorts. "I'd take a hydra on solid ground over winged predators when we have no ranged weapons or battle magic."

"That's not all," Wilhelm adds. "They spoke about the second round. A notorious troll mercenary named Velgos. They claim he's fought in this coliseum before and rivals the undefeated champion."

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_threatened == 2 && v_b2_ch4_inform == 0) {
"Did anyone happen to overhear the guards talking about the matchups?" I ask.

"As a matter of fact, yes," Wilhelm says. "Last night I overheard two patrolmen mention harpies and manticores for the opening round. Might have heard gargoyles too, though I'm not certain."

"Better than expected," Bruce grunts. "Gargoyles are rough, but they're nothing like facing a hydra."

"A hydra doesn't fly," the mage retorts. "I'd take a hydra on solid ground over winged predators when we have no ranged weapons or battle magic."

"That's not all," Wilhelm adds. "They spoke about the second round. A notorious troll mercenary named Velgos. They claim he's fought in this coliseum before and rivals the undefeated champion."

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_threatened == 2 && v_b2_ch4_inform == 1) {
"Did anyone happen to overhear the guards talking about the matchups?" I ask.

Seeing that I've decided against sharing the cavern findings, Leila casts a disappointed glance my way, but keeps quiet.

"As a matter of fact, yes," Wilhelm says. "Last night I overheard two patrolmen mention harpies and manticores for the opening round. Might have heard gargoyles too, though I'm not certain."

"Better than expected," Bruce grunts. "Gargoyles are rough, but they're nothing like facing a hydra."

"A hydra doesn't fly," the mage retorts. "I'd take a hydra on solid ground over winged predators when we have no ranged weapons or battle magic."

"That's not all," Wilhelm adds. "They spoke about the second round. A notorious troll mercenary named Velgos. They claim he's fought in this coliseum before and rivals the undefeated champion."

}
"Velgos?" Bruce frowns. "Never heard of him."

"Neither have I," the mage says.

"If a troll is their grand champion," Bruce says, "we've been giving the organizers too much credit. I've put down trolls before. Take the head off. Problem solved."

"Leave the troll for round two," the mage says. "What about the winged beasts? Anyone know their vulnerabilities?"

choice("Tell them the creatures' weaknesses", B2-Ch04a-Weaknesses, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Weaknesses, v_b2_ch4_inform3 = 1)
choice("Tell them nothing", B2-Ch04a-Weaknesses, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Weaknesses, v_b2_ch4_inform3 = 0)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Weaknesses
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch4_inform3 == 1) {
"I've spent the past few days studying creature lore," I say. "I have notes on harpy and manticore anatomy, along with their blind spots."

"What about the other beasts you read up on?" the mage asks. "Do you know their weaknesses too?"

"I'd like to hear whatever Barry has compiled," Wilhelm says.

"All right," I say. "Brace yourselves, because it's a long breakdown."

I end up walking them through far more than I intended. I cover behavioral cues, sensory ranges, and vital organs for every creature likely to enter the arena, plus several hypothetical threats. We spend the rest of the recreation block hammering out defensive formations. We even agree to a second strategy session later in the evening after everyone reviews the material in their cells.

}
#if(v_b2_ch4_inform3 == 0) {
Silence settles over the room. Surprisingly, the disgraced noble thug is the one to speak up.

"I know creature lore," he mutters. "Read about beasts in the estate library before they ran me off. Harpies and manticores had entire chapters."

"An educated highborn!" Bruce grins. "Who would have thought? Don't hold out—spill every detail. We need all the edge we can get if we're surviving tomorrow."

"For once, Bruce has a point," Wilhelm says. "Tell us everything you recall. The rest of us will contribute whatever myths and anecdotes we know."

The rest of the recreation period is spent debating the thug's notes and testing different tactics against each beast. I stay out of the conversation. I wouldn't trust any of them to guard my back in a skirmish. A few hours later, I head to Leila's room for our own private briefing, walking through the roster and running counters for each creature.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Leaving, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Leaving)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Leaving
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_threatened == 0) {
By nightfall, our counter-tactics against the troll remain unchanged: sever the head, isolate or destroy it, and pray the theory holds.

Back in my cell, I drop onto the cot and sleep hard.

At dawn, a guard's fist hammers against the iron door, barking that we move in twenty minutes. I pack my gear, cinch the straps, and step out into the recreation room where the rest of our division is already assembling. Once everyone reports, we file past the threshold into the corridor, handing our cell keys to an arena attendant who gestures for us to follow.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_threatened == 1) {
By nightfall, our counter-tactics against the troll remain unchanged: sever the head, isolate or destroy it, and pray the theory holds.

Back in my cell, I lie staring at the stone ceiling, my thoughts circling back to the vision of Wilhelm driving a blade into my spine during the melee.

I know his play now. As long as I keep him in my peripheral vision, he won't get the angle. With that settled, I close my eyes and sleep.

At dawn, a guard's fist hammers against the iron door, barking that we move in twenty minutes. I pack my gear, cinch the straps, and step out into the recreation room where the rest of our division is already assembling. Once everyone reports, we file past the threshold into the corridor, handing our cell keys to an arena attendant who gestures for us to follow.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_lessathi == 0 && v_b2_ch2_threatened == 2) {
By nightfall, our counter-tactics against the troll remain unchanged: sever the head, isolate or destroy it, and pray the theory holds.

Back in my cell, I lie staring at the stone ceiling, my thoughts circling back to the vision of Wilhelm driving a blade into my spine during the melee.

He shouldn't try anything after the scare I gave him two days ago. Even if he gets ambitious, I'll be watching his hands. With that settled, I close my eyes and sleep.

At dawn, a guard's fist hammers against the iron door, barking that we move in twenty minutes. I pack my gear, cinch the straps, and step out into the recreation room where the rest of our division is already assembling. Once everyone reports, we file past the threshold into the corridor, handing our cell keys to an arena attendant who gestures for us to follow.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_lessathi == 1 && v_b2_ch2_threatened == 2) {
By nightfall, our counter-tactics against the troll remain unchanged: sever the head, isolate or destroy it, and pray the theory holds.

Back in my cell, I lie staring at the stone ceiling, my thoughts circling back to the vision of Wilhelm driving a blade into my spine during the melee.

He shouldn't try anything after the scare I gave him two days ago, but there's a chance he caught Leila's text about me being a lessathi when he spied on us before. I'll have to track his movements constantly. With that settled, I close my eyes and sleep.

At dawn, a guard's fist hammers against the iron door, barking that we move in twenty minutes. I pack my gear, cinch the straps, and step out into the recreation room where the rest of our division is already assembling. Once everyone reports, we file past the threshold into the corridor, handing our cell keys to an arena attendant who gestures for us to follow.

}
We march through the torch-lit passageways as gladiators from other divisions emerge from their barracks to join the column. On the upper level, armed guards herd us into double files to maintain control. No sign of Daren, Kate, or Hadrik in the ranks, but the roar of the city filters down through the ventilation grates—thousands of spectators swarming the gates, the ticket queues stretching around the outer colonnade.

The guards escort us through the gladiator archway into the lower staging lockers, where we stow our packs. The officer in charge announces that we can walk the gladiator perimeter beneath the stands until the opening horn, at which point all fighters must report to their assigned division pens.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Trace, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Trace)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Trace
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_kate == 0) {
This is the window to set up the reunion between Leila and Kate. I motion for Leila to follow me toward the tunnel ring beneath the stands, saying only that there's someone she should meet. She gives me a guarded look, but falls into step beside me.

The walkway opens beneath the amphitheater, circling the vast sunken battleground. The fighting pit spans three times the size of standard arena floors in imperial histories. Tier upon tier of stone seating towers above us, built to seat thirty thousand spectators, and nearly every bench is already filling. Shouts, drums, and vendors' calls echo off the masonry as the city packs in for the spectacle.

We weave through clusters of pacing fighters, checking faces.

Half an hour passes with no trace of our friends. Daren's transceiver fails to connect—too much magical interference and overlapping signals from the crowds overhead. The opening horn could blow any second.

"Leila?" a voice cuts through the din behind us. "Leila... is that you?"

Leila stops dead and turns. The color drains from her face.

"Kate?..." glowing blue letters flicker in the air before dissolving, cut short as Kate rushes forward and pulls Leila into a fierce, desperate embrace.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_kate == 1) {
This is the window to set up the reunion between Leila and Kate. I tell Leila about the plan, and after a moment of hesitation, she falls into step beside me.

The walkway opens beneath the amphitheater, circling the vast sunken battleground. The fighting pit spans three times the size of standard arena floors in imperial histories. Tier upon tier of stone seating towers above us, built to seat thirty thousand spectators, and nearly every bench is already filling. Shouts, drums, and vendors' calls echo off the masonry as the city packs in for the spectacle.

We weave through clusters of pacing fighters, checking faces.

Half an hour passes with no trace of our friends. Daren's transceiver fails to connect—too much magical interference and overlapping signals from the crowds overhead. The opening horn could blow any second.

"Leila?" a voice cuts through the din behind us. "Leila... is that you?"

Leila stops dead and turns, her fingers hesitating in midair.

"Kate, I..." blue text flickers in the air, but Kate rushes forward and pulls Leila into a fierce, desperate embrace.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Safe, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Safe)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Safe
TEXT:

"Leila!" Kate's voice breaks. "By the gods, I thought you were dead. They told us the experiments killed you. Oh gods, Leila, I'm so sorry. I shouldn't have believed them! I should have searched for you the way I searched for the others."

"You don't need to apolo—" Leila begins writing, but stops when she sees Kate squeeze her eyes shut, fighting back tears.

"I abandoned you," Kate says, her throat tight. "Please forgive me. I was so consumed with finding Diane that I—"

A deafening brass horn blasts across the coliseum, vibrating the stone beneath our boots. The tournament is starting.

"Kate, we have to move," Daren says from behind her.

Daren and Hadrik stand just past the archway—they must have linked up earlier to search the corridors together.

"I know," Kate says, dragging a sleeve across her eyes.

She turns to me, her expression hardening with fierce resolve.

"Please... keep her safe."

"I will," I say.

"Kate, wait, I..." Leila writes, her mouth parted as if to speak aloud, but Kate is already walking briskly toward her division's gate.

choice("Call after Kate", B2-Ch04a-Reunion, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Reunion, v_b2_ch4_called_kate = 1)
choice("There's no time. The two of them can talk more after the arena.", B2-Ch04a-Reunion, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Reunion, v_b2_ch4_called_kate = 0)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Reunion
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_kate == 0 && v_b2_ch4_called_kate == 1) {
"Kate!" I yell, loud enough to cut through the roar of the stands.

Kate halts and glances back. Seeing Leila frozen, I nudge her elbow. She catches on instantly, and massive blue characters blaze in the air between them:

"Kate, I'm sorry too. I thought you wouldn't want to see me because I'm a lessathi! Now that we've both apologized, let's win our rounds and get out alive so we can keep apologizing properly!"

Kate stares at the glowing words, stunned for a beat, before a genuine smile breaks across her face.

"It's a promise!" she calls back, then turns and sprints for her gate.

"Give 'em hell, Barry!" Hadrik barks with a wide grin, heading off toward his pen.

"Better yet, don't die," Daren says, catching my arm. "Survival is the objective, not glory. We regroup after the final bout. Nothing reckless. Stay alive. Understood?"

"Understood," I say.

Daren nods and heads for the third division pen. Leila and I turn back toward our own waiting area.

"So," I say, matching her stride, "glad you came along to meet my friends?"

Leila blinks, realizing only now that Kate was the person I went searching for all along.

"Yes," her text forms, a soft smile on her face. "I am. Thank you..."

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_kate == 1 && v_b2_ch4_called_kate == 1) {
"Kate!" I yell, loud enough to cut through the roar of the stands.

Kate halts and glances back. Seeing Leila frozen, I nudge her elbow. She catches on instantly, and massive blue characters blaze in the air between them:

"Kate, I'm sorry too. I thought you wouldn't want to see me because I'm a lessathi! Now that we've both apologized, let's win our rounds and get out alive so we can keep apologizing properly!"

Kate stares at the glowing words, stunned for a beat, before a genuine smile breaks across her face.

"It's a promise!" she calls back, then turns and sprints for her gate.

"Give 'em hell, Barry!" Hadrik barks with a wide grin, heading off toward his pen.

"Better yet, don't die," Daren says, catching my arm. "Survival is the objective, not glory. We regroup after the final bout. Nothing reckless. Stay alive. Understood?"

"Understood," I say.

Daren nods and heads for the third division pen. Leila and I turn back toward our own waiting area.

"So," I say, matching her stride, "glad you came along to meet Kate after all?"

Leila offers a faint smile.

"Yes..." she writes. "Yes, I am. Thank you..."

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_kate == 0 && v_b2_ch4_called_kate == 0) {
I let Kate go. There will be time enough to talk once the blood dries on the sands.

"Give 'em hell, Barry!" Hadrik barks with a wide grin, heading off toward his pen.

"Better yet, don't die," Daren says, catching my arm. "Survival is the objective, not glory. We regroup after the final bout. Nothing reckless. Stay alive. Understood?"

"Understood," I say.

Daren nods and heads for the third division pen. Leila and I turn back toward our own waiting area.

She walks in silence, her gaze distant.

"Don't worry," I tell her. "You'll have all the time you want with Kate once we're done here."

"Yes..." Leila writes. "Of course..."

"So... glad you came along to meet my friends?"

Leila blinks, realizing only now that Kate was the person I went searching for all along.

"Yes," her text forms, a soft smile on her face. "I am. Thank you..."

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_kate == 1 && v_b2_ch4_called_kate == 0) {
