choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Private, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Private)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Private
TEXT:

I lock the screen and slide the stat booster back into my pocket. Leila does the same, tucking hers away. Knowing her tendencies, she probably dumped everything into toughness. I'd ask, but not with our division listening.

"Found a way to pass secret notes, did you?" Bruce grunts, eyeing the devices. "Hope you finished saying your goodbyes. We're on in minutes."

A second horn blares across the coliseum—the official commencement. Stragglers scramble for open benches in the upper tiers. The announcer clears his throat through the magnifier, his voice echoing off every stone facade and drowning out the crowd's murmur.

"Welcome," the announcer booms. "Welcome, citizens and guests of Ollendor! No lengthy preamble is necessary today. You came for the spectacle of the century, and the arena delivers. Before the gates lift, hear the official decree of the games."

He pauses, surveying the coliseum, then unrolls a parchment.

"By order of Master Venard, owner of the arena, today's tournament observes the ancient rules—instituted by his forebears and drawn from the pre-Still Winter Lessathi codes. Under these provisions, breaking a combat regulation does not bring immediate disqualification. Instead, the violator is branded an undesirable. An undesirable must face all surviving gladiators simultaneously in a penalty bout following the official rounds. Any combatant who delivers the killing strike to an undesirable earns immediate freedom or substantial gold. Gladiators may decline to participate in the penalty melee, and undesirables may withdraw from standard rounds once branded."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Undesirables, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Undesirables)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Undesirables
TEXT:

The announcer coughs and turns the parchment.

"Standard arena laws remain absolute. Any gladiator who casts unauthorized magic, strikes at teammates, or threatens the spectator stands will be executed on the spot. The undesirable designation applies solely to tournament format violations. The games consist of three rounds. Round one: a gauntlet against mythical beasts never before seen in this coliseum. Round two brings a special engagement: in place of our reigning champion, the arena presents the feared northern mercenary, Velgos the Troll!"

A collective gasp ripples through the stands, followed by excited mutters. His reputation clearly carried ahead of him.

"In round three," the announcer continues, "the surviving gladiators will be paired in duels to determine the ultimate victors—some to surrender, some to the death, determined by the roster of survivors. With the rules established, I ask you all: are you ready?"

The coliseum erupts in a deafening roar of cheers and stamping feet.

"Then let the games begin!" the announcer shouts over the din.

Below the royal pavilion, a massive portcullis grinds upward on heavy iron chains.

"For round one, divisions will enter the arena in numerical order. Give a warm reception to the first division!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Manticores, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Manticores)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Manticores
TEXT:

Applause and whistles wash down from the stands as the first division files out into the glaring sunlight of the arena pit. I don't recognize any of them, but from the cheers, several must be seasoned arena fighters. A couple of them swagger toward the center, weapons raised to milk the applause. Overconfident. Bruce wasn't exaggerating—whatever they survived before won't prepare them for what's behind those gates.

Across the sand, the heavy southern portcullis screeches open.

A winged monstrosity bounds through—the body of a massive black-maned lion, bat-like leathery wings, curved bull horns, glowing red eyes, and a segmented scorpion tail arching over its spine. The manticore doesn't hesitate. With two powerful wingbeats, it launches into the air. Two more manticores burst from the gate behind it, banking wide to circle the open sky above the arena. Murmurs of alarm ripple through the lower rows as the beasts soar close to the barricades.

"Do not be alarmed!" the announcer calls through the magnifier. "The beasts are conditioned to engage only the combatants on the sand!"

From the same dark archway, several giant scorpions scuttle onto the arena floor, advancing in a tight, methodical line toward the gladiators.

#if(v_b2_ch2_heard == 1 && v_b2_ch4_inform2 == 0 && v_b2_ch4_inform3 == 0) {
Good thing I caught the guards talking about scorpions while locked in my cell. I had time to prepare and briefed Leila on their weak points.

At least against scorpions we have an angle... Wait. Those aren't giant scorpions. Smaller head, segmented dorsal plates, darker carapace. Desert marauders. The guards couldn't tell the difference between species.

Calm down. A desert marauder shares most predatory habits with giant scorpions, but its dorsal armor is thicker. The nerve cluster is under one of the rear plates, but which one? If only I had my notebook...

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_heard == 1 && v_b2_ch4_inform2 == 1 || v_b2_ch2_heard == 1 && v_b2_ch4_inform3 == 1) {
Good thing I caught the guards talking about scorpions while locked in my cell. I had time to prepare and briefed the entire division on their weak points.

At least against scorpions we have an angle... Wait. Those aren't giant scorpions. Smaller head, segmented dorsal plates, darker carapace. Desert marauders. The guards couldn't tell the difference between species.

Calm down. A desert marauder shares most predatory habits with giant scorpions, but its dorsal armor is thicker. The nerve cluster is under one of the rear plates, but which one? If only I had my notebook...

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_heard == 0) {
Wait. Those aren't giant scorpions. Smaller head, segmented dorsal plates, darker carapace. Desert marauders. I logged their anatomy in my notebook—the fatal vulnerability sits under one of the dorsal plates, but I can't recall which segment. If only I had my notebook...

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Backpack, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Backpack)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Backpack
TEXT:

Should I go fetch it? We're fourth in order, so there's plenty of time before our gate lifts. But leaving means missing the opening bouts. In a tournament like this, watching the creatures fight under arena conditions could keep me breathing.

#if(v_ch6_secure == 1) {
If only I hadn't had Daren secure my pack, I could send Leila to grab the pages. Pointless to regret it now. Two options: leave to read the notes, or stay and watch the sand.

}
#if(v_ch6_secure == 0) {
There's another angle. I could send Leila with the locker key to tear out the relevant pages. But could she find them quickly? I indexed the contents, but my handwriting isn't known for clarity. She can't bring the entire book either—she might get caught carrying it onto the sand. Time is short. I need to decide.

}
choice("Stay here and watch the fights.", B2-Ch04a-Remain, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Remain, v_b2_ch4_backpack = 1) if (v_b2_ch2_heard == 0)
choice("Stay here and watch the fights.", B2-Ch04a-Remain, v_ac_b2_ch4_risk = 1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Remain, v_b2_ch4_backpack = 1) if (v_b2_ch2_heard == 1)
choice("Go read the notebook.", B2-Ch04a-Read, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Read, v_b2_ch4_backpack = 2)
choice("Send Leila to bring me the notebook pages.", B2-Ch04a-Remain, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Remain, v_b2_ch4_backpack = 3) if (v_ch6_secure == 0)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Read
TEXT:

#if(v_ch6_secure == 1) {
I'll check the notebook myself. Safer that way.

}
#if(v_ch6_secure == 0) {
I'll check the notebook myself. Safer that way.

}
"Leila," I say. "I need to check my notes in the lockers. Track the fight patterns while I'm gone."

Leila nods.

"Back in five minutes," I say.

I slip out of our division pen and head down the corridor toward the lockers. A guard steps into my path before I make ten paces.

"Hold it," the guard grunts. "Where do you think you're going?"

"Forgot gear in my locker," I say. "Just retrieving it."

"Gladiators don't leave their staging pens without written clearance."

"Fine," I say. "Who grants clearance?"

He scratches his chin, blinking as if no one ever asked before.

"Stay right there," he says. "Don't move a muscle until I get a pass from the director, or you'll take iron to the ribs."

He heads up the stairs and vanishes. A few minutes later, he returns with a stamped scrap of parchment.

"Pass signed by Master Venard," he says, thrusting it into my hand. "Show it to any guard who stops you. When you return, hand it straight back to me. Understood?"

"Understood," I say, and hurry toward the locker rooms.

At my locker, I turn the key and pull the notebook from my pack. I tear out the section on desert marauders and winged predators, stash the book, and lock the compartment.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Himself, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Himself)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Himself
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_heard == 1) {
Skimming the notes on the jog back, the anatomy is nearly identical to standard giant scorpions—vital nerve center tucked under the third dorsal segment. The sprint was essentially wasted effort.

I hand the pass back to the sentry at the corridor checkpoint, then slip back through our pen's doorway to the iron grating.

Both the first and second division bouts are already over. Manticores, scorpions, and strange spined porcupines lie in heaps across the bloody sand. In the center of the pit, Hadrik's division teammates are tossing him into the air, chanting his name at the top of their lungs while the stands roar.

"What did I miss?" I ask Leila.

"Your dwarf friend cleared both waves alone," Leila writes.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_heard == 0) {
Skimming the notes on the jog back, I locate the vital nerve center tucked under the third dorsal segment.

I hand the pass back to the sentry at the corridor checkpoint, then slip back through our pen's doorway to the iron grating.

Both the first and second division bouts are already over. Manticores, scorpions, and strange spined porcupines lie in heaps across the bloody sand. In the center of the pit, Hadrik's division teammates are tossing him into the air, chanting his name at the top of their lungs while the stands roar.

"What did I miss?" I ask Leila.

"Your dwarf friend cleared both waves alone," Leila writes.

}
"Alone?" I stare. "What about division one?"

"Wiped out," Leila writes, pointing toward the far wall where several mangled corpses lie half-hidden beneath a dead marauder.

"Damn," I mutter. "Third division is up next, then us. Get ready."

It takes another minute for the cheering to subside as Hadrik and his ecstatic teammates exit through the victory arch.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Daft, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Daft)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Remain
TEXT:

achievement("A calculated risk",v_ac_b2_ch4_risk)
#if(v_b2_ch4_backpack == 1) {
Forget the notebook. The organizers might not even deploy marauders against our division, and I need eyes on the sand.

The marauders close the distance while the three manticores circle above, watching for openings.

The first division fighters hold a disciplined skirmish line, showing no sign of panic. At a hand signal from their leader, three fighters break left to engage the flank marauder while the rest fan out to draw the other beasts. A lone warrior hangs back near the center, tracking the circling manticores to call out dives.

The scorpion-like beasts tower five times the height of the men, their heavy chitinous claws capable of snapping a torso in two. But the gladiators fight with seasoned rhythm—parrying strikes, stepping inside reach, and covering each other's blind spots. The three-man squad on the left works with lethal precision, carving into the creature's leg joints to force it low.

The most agile of the trio scrambles onto the beast's armored back and drives a short blade straight into the base of the skull. The marauder screeches, its barbed tail whipping over its spine to impale him. The fighter throws himself clear just in time as his two partners surge forward, driving their broadswords deep into the creature's compound eyes.

}
#if(v_b2_ch4_backpack == 3) {
"Leila," I say in a low voice. "Need a favor."

She looks over, waiting.

"My notebook has anatomy charts on desert marauders, but I need the exact nerve locations. Take my key, open locker four, and tear out the pages on marauders. There's an index in the front. I'd go myself, but I need eyes on these fight patterns."

"Give me the key," Leila writes.

I hand her the iron key.

"Locker number is stamped on the handle. You know the leather binding, right?"

"I know it," she writes. "Back in five minutes."

She slips out into the corridor.

Out on the sand, the marauders have closed the distance while the manticores continue their wide circles overhead.

The first division fighters hold a disciplined skirmish line, showing no sign of panic. At a hand signal from their leader, three fighters break left to engage the flank marauder while the rest fan out to draw the other beasts. A lone warrior hangs back near the center, tracking the circling manticores to call out dives.

The scorpion-like beasts tower five times the height of the men, their heavy chitinous claws capable of snapping a torso in two. But the gladiators fight with seasoned rhythm—parrying strikes, stepping inside reach, and covering each other's blind spots. The three-man squad on the left works with lethal precision, carving into the creature's leg joints to force it low.

The most agile of the trio scrambles onto the beast's armored back and drives a short blade straight into the base of the skull. The marauder screeches, its barbed tail whipping over its spine to impale him. The fighter throws himself clear just in time as his two partners surge forward, driving their broadswords deep into the creature's compound eyes.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Mercy, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Mercy)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Mercy
TEXT:

The stands erupt in roars as the massive carcass crashes into the sand, black hemolymph spraying from its ruined eye sockets. The three fighters raise bloodied swords to salute the crowd, then turn to reinforce their frontline.

"Fall back!" the spotter screams, pointing upward. "Fall back now!"

The gladiators look up, but it's already too late.

With folded wings, all three manticores plummet like stones. In a blur of black leather and razor talons, they slam into the three victors, sinking curved claws through chainmail and bone before kicking back into the air with their prey.

Blood-curdling shrieks silence thirty thousand spectators. Two gladiators drop their weapons from the agony, their chests shredded. The third grips his hilt with both hands and thrusts upward into the beast's soft underbelly. The manticore howls, releasing its grip. The warrior plummets fifty feet, smashing skull-first into the packed earth with a sickening crunch. Seconds later, the other two beasts drop their limp prey from even higher, the bodies breaking on impact.

Panic shatters the remaining fighters. Their leader is dead on the sand. Without commands, their formation fractures. A marauder seizes one man in its left pincer, lifting him screaming before its tail drives through his ribcage.

The southern portcullis screeches again. Two more manticores sprint into the arena, eyes burning crimson. The spotter goes down first—torn to shreds before he can draw a blade. The remaining three fighters are surrounded, pincers crushing limbs while barbed tails and fangs finish the butchery in seconds.

When the dust settles, the coliseum sits in stunned, suffocating silence, broken only by the wet tearing of flesh as the beasts feed.

"By the gods..." Bruce whispers, his knuckles white against our iron grating.

"It appears our reigning champions have fallen," the announcer's voice echoes, laced with manufactured tension. "A grim omen for the divisions to come. Will today's games become an absolute slaughter?"

The second division's gate groans open.

No one steps out. After ten agonizing seconds of silence, a frantic scream echoes from the tunnel.

"I'm not going out there!" a man shrieks. "Kill me here if you want! You saw what happened to them! We don't stand a chance!"

"March onto the sand," a guard's voice growls, "or I'll give you a death that makes being eaten alive look like mercy."

"No—please!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Trash, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Trash)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Trash
TEXT:

The argument between the panicked gladiator and the guards carries clearly across the sands. Uneasy mutters break out in the lower tiers.

"Technical delay," the announcer's voice booms through the magnifier. "Remain seated, ladies and gentlemen. Our personnel will have the program under way in—"

The announcer cuts off.

From the second division archway, a single dwarf strolls out onto the sand, an earthenware bottle clutched in one hand. The crowd and the announcer fall dead silent as Hadrik saunters toward the center of the pit, tips his head back, and chugs the remaining dregs in three massive swallows.

"Ah!" Hadrik exhales with gusto, wiping foam from his black beard. "Nothing like northern ale to prime the blood for a scrap!"

He looks around the empty sand, scratching his neck.

"Say, you wouldn't happen to have a bin around here?" he yells toward the announcer's box, holding up the empty jug. "Trash barrel? Basket? Hate to leave broken pottery everywhere."

The announcer stares down in total bewilderment.

"No? A crate? Paper sack?"

Behind him, a desert marauder creeps forward, raising its heavy left pincer to snap his spine.

"Never mind!" Hadrik laughs. "Improvised club it is!"

He pivots on his heel and cracks the heavy bottle straight across the marauder's snout. The jug shatters into ceramic shards that tear into the creature's compound eyes. As the beast thrashes, Hadrik leaps, driving an aura-hardened right fist down onto its skull. The impact slams the beast into the earth with a concussive shockwave, carving a crater in the packed dirt.

A manticore dives from the sky, talons bared for his throat. Hadrik sways half a step aside at the final instant, catches the beast by the base of its scorpion tail, spins twice to build crushing centrifugal force, and hurls the monstrosity across the arena. It slams into the reinforced stone perimeter wall with a thunderous crash, shattering masonry and collapsing in a heap of broken limbs.

Silence grips the amphitheater. Thirty thousand people stare in utter disbelief.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Porcupines, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Porcupines)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Porcupines
TEXT:

"Don't fret about that masonry!" Hadrik calls up cheerfully, mistaking the stunned hush for concern. "Checked behind it earlier—just an empty tool shed. Nobody's hurt!"

A deafening roar tears through the coliseum—the entire crowd surging to its feet, stomping and bellowing at full throat.

"Now that's what I'm talking about!" Hadrik roars back, his face splitting into a broad grin. "That's an arena crowd! Hold on to your tankards—the party's just warming up!"

He charges the next marauder and plants a brutal forehead headbutt straight between its mandibles, dropping the beast instantly.

"Deploy the second wave!" the announcer yells over the noise.

The southern portcullis lifts wider. More desert marauders surge through, followed by a pack of stocky, quadrupedal beasts bristling with long, steel-hard spines.

Archer porcupines. Their quills aren't ordinary keratin; they're dense, razor-sharp natural projectiles capable of punching through tempered armor. Under rapid cellular regeneration, spent quills regrow within seconds, giving them virtually limitless ammunition.

Several porcupines fan out thirty paces behind Hadrik, pivoting to present their bristling spines. Hadrik is locked in close combat with a marauder and hasn't seen the flankers line up.

choice("Warn him", B2-Ch04a-Lullaby, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Lullaby, v_b2_ch4_beer = 1)
choice("Don't warn him", B2-Ch04a-Lullaby, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Lullaby, v_b2_ch4_beer = 0)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Lullaby
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch4_beer == 1) {
"Hadrik!" I shout through the grating. "Behind you!"

Hadrik glances over his shoulder, spots the bristling quills, and holds his stance until the volley launches. He drops low at the split second of release. Six heavy quills whistle over his head, thudding deep into the marauder in front of him. The dwarf closes the distance before the beasts can reload, hammering the porcupines with rapid, bone-crushing punches until the cluster lies motionless.

"Good eye, Barry!" Hadrik calls out with a grin. "I owe you a beer."

The quill-riddled marauder collapses in the dirt, dead.

Overhead, the manticores break off from circling and wheel for position. One dives toward Hadrik's back, but pulls up sharply the moment the dwarf turns, clawing back into the upper thermals.

"Coward!" Hadrik bellows, shaking a fist at the sky. "Get down here and take your beating!"

"Hey, dwarf!" Arraka's screeching voice rings out from the spectator seats. "Sing them a lullaby! Maybe they'll nap on the sand! Hahahahahaha!"

I trace the cackle to the lower rows. Flower sits among the crowd, everyone around her staring in confusion at the bronze amulet at her throat.

"Shut up, you old hag!" Hadrik hollers back. "I'm doubling your spinning routine tonight!"

"Stop wasting my afternoon and kill them!" Arraka snarls from the amulet. "Do you know what these tickets cost?"

"They're flying, you lunatic!" Hadrik shouts. "I don't have ranged weapons!"

"Then throw the spears!" Arraka snaps. "Do I have to do all your thinking? There are spears mounted along the perimeter wall. Throw them!"

Along the stone barrier, weapons hang in iron brackets for unarmed prisoners. Hadrik grunts, rips a heavy boar spear from its mount, pivots, and hurls it. The iron tip punches straight through a manticore's chest, bringing the beast crashing down in a spray of gore. Before it hits the dirt, Hadrik grabs two more spears and launches them in rapid succession, pinning two more manticores out of the sky.

}
#if(v_b2_ch4_beer == 0) {
He knows what he's doing.

As Hadrik crushes the marauder's skull, three porcupines loose their volleys. Six steel-tipped quills thud solidly into the dwarf's bare back.

"Huh," Hadrik grunts, sounding like a man who just caught a gnat.

He reaches over his shoulders, plucks the iron-hard quills out one by one, and tosses them aside. The tips barely broke the outer layer of his giant-toughened skin; hardly a drop of blood wells up. Natural giant resilience shrugs off unenchanted steel.

Before the porcupines can chamber another volley, Hadrik closes the gap, shattering skulls and ribs with heavy fists until the pack lies still.

Overhead, the manticores break off from circling and wheel for position. One dives toward Hadrik's back, but pulls up sharply the moment the dwarf turns, clawing back into the upper thermals.

"Coward!" Hadrik bellows, shaking a fist at the sky. "Get down here and take your beating!"

"Hey, dwarf!" Arraka's screeching voice rings out from the spectator seats. "Sing them a lullaby! Maybe they'll nap on the sand! Hahahahahaha!"

I trace the cackle to the lower rows. Flower sits among the crowd, everyone around her staring in confusion at the bronze amulet at her throat.

"Shut up, you old hag!" Hadrik hollers back. "I'm doubling your spinning routine tonight!"

"Stop wasting my afternoon and kill them!" Arraka snarls from the amulet. "Do you know what these tickets cost?"

"They're flying, you lunatic!" Hadrik shouts. "I don't have ranged weapons!"

"Then throw the spears!" Arraka snaps. "Do I have to do all your thinking? There are spears mounted along the perimeter wall. Throw them!"

Along the stone barrier, weapons hang in iron brackets for unarmed prisoners. Hadrik grunts, rips a heavy boar spear from its mount, pivots, and hurls it. The iron tip punches straight through a manticore's chest, bringing the beast crashing down in a spray of gore. Before it hits the dirt, Hadrik grabs two more spears and launches them in rapid succession, pinning two more manticores out of the sky.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Chanting, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Chanting)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Chanting
TEXT:

set(v_b2_ch4_hearing1,0) if (v_hearing < 3)
set(v_b2_ch4_hearing1,1) if (v_hearing >= 3)
#if(v_hearing < 3) {
The last surviving manticore folds its wings and dives straight at Hadrik in a desperate, frantic plunge. Hadrik times the descent, leaps eight feet into the air, intercepts the beast by the neck, and drives it headfirst into the sand in a crushing power slam.

He took both waves completely alone, barely sporting a scratch.

The arena explodes. Hadrik's division mates scramble from the tunnel, surround him, and hoist him onto their shoulders, chanting his name at the top of their lungs.

"Hadrik! Hadrik! Hadrik!"

"Don't think this excuses you cowards," Hadrik grins as they toss him upward. "You're covering my tab for the next year!"

The cheering rumbles on for another two minutes before Hadrik and his division clear the sand.

}
#if(v_hearing >= 3) {
The last surviving manticore folds its wings and dives straight at Hadrik in a desperate, frantic plunge. Hadrik times the descent, leaps eight feet into the air, intercepts the beast by the neck, and drives it headfirst into the sand in a crushing power slam.

He took both waves completely alone, barely sporting a scratch.

The arena explodes. Hadrik's division mates scramble from the tunnel, surround him, and hoist him onto their shoulders, chanting his name at the top of their lungs.

"Hadrik! Hadrik! Hadrik!"

"Don't think this excuses you cowards," Hadrik grins as they toss him upward. "You're covering my tab for the next year!"

"What is the meaning of this?!" the Ambassador of Ollendor's voice snarls from the private gallery above our pen.

"Ambassador!" Master Venard replies smoothly. "You decided to attend after all. Wine?"

The two converse in the private box directly overhead; my enhanced hearing picks up every syllable.

"Why is that dwarf in the pit?" the ambassador demands. "He wasn't part of the arrangement! He will ruin everything!"

"Calm yourself," Venard says. "The dwarf arrived with an entry writ bearing the king's royal seal. We could hardly turn him away."

"Turning him away is one thing; letting him make a mockery of the arena is another!"

"Mockery?" Venard chuckles. "He just handed thirty thousand spectators a performance they'll remember for decades. Ticket sales for the finals just tripled. The games are already an overwhelming success."

"I don't care about your ledger!" the ambassador snaps. "Remove him. I will not have him on that sand again!"

"Hmm," Venard murmurs. "Allowing one combatant to dominate the sand does risk rendering the later bouts predictable. Very well. I'll have the matchmasters alter the bracket. You won't see him in the pit again."

The voices fade into quiet above. Out on the sand, the roar of the crowd gradually subsides as Hadrik's division files out through the gate.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Retained, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Retained) if (v_b2_ch4_backpack == 3)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Daft, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Daft) if (v_b2_ch4_backpack != 3)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Retained
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_heard == 1) {
Minutes before the next division is called, Leila returns from the lockers, clutching torn sheets from my notebook.

"Sentry stopped me in the corridor," blue script forms. "Had to fetch a signed clearance pass before he'd let me through."

"No problem. Got everything?"

"All I could locate," Leila writes. "Anatomy charts, behavioral patterns, weak points."

"Good work."

I take the pages and scan the lines.

The anatomy is essentially identical to giant scorpions—vital nerve center under the third dorsal segment. The sprint was wasted effort.

}
#if(v_b2_ch2_heard == 0) {
Minutes before the next division is called, Leila returns from the lockers, clutching torn sheets from my notebook.

"Sentry stopped me in the corridor," blue script forms. "Had to fetch a signed clearance pass before he'd let me through."

"No problem. Got everything?"

"All I could locate," Leila writes. "Anatomy charts, behavioral patterns, weak points."

"Good work."

I take the pages and scan the lines.

The notes are precise. If we draw desert marauders in our round, targeting the third dorsal plate will drop them fast.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Daft, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Daft)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Daft
TEXT:

The third division portcullis lifts. Daren steps onto the sand first, shield raised and broadsword gleaming in the sun. His division mates file out behind him, weapons drawn, casting nervous glances toward the far gate.

"In the arena today," the announcer's voice booms over the crowd, "the scarred swordsman leading the third division is none other than Daren, the healer in armor! Renowned across the eastern continent for his valor and selfless defense of the innocent, he enters the sands today as our guest of honor. A round of applause for Daren!"

Applause ripples through thirty thousand spectators as Daren plants his boots in the center of the pit.

"Listen up," Bruce grunts, turning back toward our circle. "After seeing how the first two divisions fought, we need to adapt our strategy."

"Are you daft?" the disgraced noble snaps. "You want to overhaul our tactics with five minutes on the clock?"

"Because the way we are, we die," Bruce says bluntly. "We have zero coordination. We need structure like the first division—roles, sectors, discipline."

"The first division is vulture feed," the mage mutters.

"You know what I mean!" Bruce's voice rises. "Assign specific jobs. Leader calls targets, spotter tracks the air, decoys pull aggro, heavy hitters execute. Organized."

choice(""I think that's a good idea."", B2-Ch04a-Good, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Good)
choice(""That sounds like a terrible idea."", B2-Ch04a-Terrible, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Terrible)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Good
TEXT:

"I think that's a good idea."

"If everyone has an assigned role, panic won't shatter the line," I add.

"Oh really?" the mage sneers. "That worked wonders for division one until their shot-caller took talons to the throat. The second he dropped, the rest were meat."

"Better than entering the pit in total chaos from the jump!" Bruce barks.

"And who commands?" the noble thug sneers. "You? Don't make me laugh. I'd rather take a manticore stinger than follow your orders."

"We vote on a leader," Bruce snaps.

"Forget it," the mage says. "I'm not staking my throat on strangers. Not happening."

Across the sand, the third division bout begins.

"Release the tramplers!" the announcer roars.

Massive four-legged beasts thunder onto the sand—half-bull, half-rhinoceros, plated in bone-hard horn armor with spiked mace tails swinging behind them. Their hides are thick enough to deflect mundane iron, built to crush infantry by sheer momentum.

They lock onto the gladiators and charge at full gallop.

"Form on me!" Daren commands his squad. "Stay behind the shield!"

His teammates take one look at several tons of charging bone and horn, break rank, and sprint for the walls in blind terror.

Daren lets out a heavy breath, sets his stance, and waits. The leading trampler bears down on him. At the last second, Daren pivots inside the horns, his broadsword sweeping across the beast's neck in a clean, fluid arc. The severed head tumbles through the dirt; the massive carcass skids forward on its chest, throwing up plumes of sand before grinding to a halt.

"Gods damn it all," Bruce groans behind our grate. "See? We have to pull together or we're finished."

"Don't lose your nerve, Bruce," Wilhelm says, his voice placid and cold. "We reviewed the creature weaknesses yesterday. Keep survival at the front of your mind, and the training will carry us."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Concentrate, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Concentrate)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Terrible
TEXT:

"That sounds like a terrible idea."

"Division one had drilled together for months," I say. "The seven of us refused to exchange names until the owner threatened our lives yesterday. You don't build battlefield trust in five minutes."

"We try or we die!" Bruce insists.

"And who commands?" the noble thug sneers. "You? Don't make me laugh. I'd rather take a manticore stinger than follow your orders."

"We vote on a leader," Bruce snaps.

"Forget it," the mage says. "I'm not staking my throat on strangers. Not happening."

Across the sand, the third division bout begins.

"Release the tramplers!" the announcer roars.

Massive four-legged beasts thunder onto the sand—half-bull, half-rhinoceros, plated in bone-hard horn armor with spiked mace tails swinging behind them. Their hides are thick enough to deflect mundane iron, built to crush infantry by sheer momentum.

They lock onto the gladiators and charge at full gallop.

"Form on me!" Daren commands his squad. "Stay behind the shield!"

His teammates take one look at several tons of charging bone and horn, break rank, and sprint for the walls in blind terror.

Daren lets out a heavy breath, sets his stance, and waits. The leading trampler bears down on him. At the last second, Daren pivots inside the horns, his broadsword sweeping across the beast's neck in a clean, fluid arc. The severed head tumbles through the dirt; the massive carcass skids forward on its chest, throwing up plumes of sand before grinding to a halt.

"Gods damn it all," Bruce groans behind our grate. "See? We have to pull together or we're finished."

"Don't lose your nerve, Bruce," Wilhelm says, his voice placid and cold. "We reviewed the creature weaknesses yesterday. Keep survival at the front of your mind, and the training will carry us."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Concentrate, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Concentrate)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Concentrate
TEXT:

The surviving tramplers wheel around the perimeter and charge back toward Daren. Most of his teammates dodge clear, but two are caught against the wall, crushed under iron hooves before they can roll away. Daren plants his boots, timing the charge with clinical precision, and drives his blade up through the underbelly, bisecting the charging beast mid-stride.

The crowd roars in appreciation.

"Deploy wave two!" the announcer calls.

Three giant cobras slither from the southern gate, hoods flared wider than a man is tall, black markings ringing their throats.

Acid spitters. While weaker than concentrated battle magic, their venom eats through forged iron in minutes and liquefies flesh on contact.

#if(v_b2_ch4_inform2 == 0 && v_b2_ch4_inform3 == 0) {
Facing ranged fire, Daren closes the distance behind his heavy heater shield. Jets of caustic green acid hiss against the enchanted metal, sizzling and running off harmlessly like rainwater.

Daren explodes into a forward sprint, severing the first cobra's head in a single diagonal cut. As the other two strike, he slams his shield into one hood, shattering fangs, while running his sword through the roof of the second snake's mouth. He pivots cleanly, finishing the stunned third serpent with a backhanded stroke.

Across the sand, the surviving gladiators manage to bring down the last trampler. Daren jogs over to secure the perimeter.

"If nobody's coordinating, I fight alone!" Bruce barks behind us. "I'm not dying for dead weight!"

"No one expected your assistance," Wilhelm murmurs smoothly. "Keep quiet. Our bout begins in moments, and I need focus."

Our squad is fracturing before we even touch the sand. Relying on these people is suicide.

}
#if(v_b2_ch4_inform2 == 1 || v_b2_ch4_inform3 == 1) {
Facing ranged fire, Daren closes the distance behind his heavy heater shield. Jets of caustic green acid hiss against the enchanted metal, sizzling and running off harmlessly like rainwater.

Daren explodes into a forward sprint, severing the first cobra's head in a single diagonal cut. As the other two strike, he slams his shield into one hood, shattering fangs, while running his sword through the roof of the second snake's mouth. He pivots cleanly, finishing the stunned third serpent with a backhanded stroke.

Across the sand, the surviving gladiators manage to bring down the last trampler. Daren jogs over to secure the perimeter.

"If nobody's coordinating, I fight alone!" Bruce barks behind us. "I'm not dying for dead weight!"

"No one expected your assistance," Wilhelm murmurs smoothly. "Keep quiet. Our bout begins in moments, and I need focus."

Our squad is fracturing before we even touch the sand. Relying on these people is suicide.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch04a-Silence, v_current_scene = B2-Ch04a-Silence)


ID: B2-Ch04a-Silence
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch2_deal == 1) {
A thunderous cheer rolls across the amphitheater as Daren and the survivors finish off the remaining beasts. The matchmaster signals them to clear the sand.

As they exit, footsteps approach from the corridor behind me. A figure in a dark blue hooded robe steps up to our grating.

"Who are you?" I ask in a low voice.

"A last-minute transfer to your division," the man says, twin sapphire-hilted scythes strapped across his back. "Sent to resolve your difficulties."

"The Lessathi sent you?" I whisper, checking that the others aren't listening. "What's the plan? You expect to take the entire wave alone?"

"I will fulfill our agreement," the operative says. "See that you honor yours."

"Right," I say. "Of course."

He snaps his head toward me, eyes narrowing with sudden, lethal fury.

"You are lying!"

"What? No, I'm—"

"Fool!" the Lessathi snarls, hand dropping to a scythe hilt. "Anyone trained in aura-reading can feel your aura trembling like a reed. You have no idea what we do to oath-breakers. I should open your throat right here."

"What's going on over there?" Bruce demands, stepping between us. "Who are you?"

The Lessathi pauses, reining in his temper. A public brawl before the gate lifts serves no one.

"The arrangement is void," he murmurs, stepping back into the shadows of the corridor. "Enjoy your execution."

He vanishes down the passageway.

"Who the hell was that?" Bruce grunts.

"No idea," I say.

Leila shoots me a piercing, suspicious look, but writes nothing.

The iron portcullis groans upward.

We file out onto the blood-soaked sand, stepping over crushed carapaces and severed limbs toward the center of the pit.

"Ladies and gentlemen—the fourth division!" the announcer booms.

The crowd falls dead silent. Nobody recognizes a single face in our lineup. To thirty thousand spectators, we're just the next batch of meat for the grinder.

Time to prove them wrong.

}
